{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ep4BBCfoQ9X5A4aqq5RewY","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nslug: pose-review-declared-threat-model\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-23\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on: \nremediates:\npriority: 4\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: feature\nsurface: standard\ndelivers: capability:review-declared-threat-model\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nLet a spec declare the accepted residual limits of a guarantee it makes, and let the review brief treat findings inside those limits as follow-ups to classify, not blockers to chase.\n\n### Business value\nEach of the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks` found a more remote adversarial variant: concurrent HEAD movement, case folding, Unicode normalization, symlinks inside submodules. Every one was real, but no round could converge, because the spec had no place to say which environment the guarantee assumes. A cap on rounds would approve something unreviewed; a declared threat model converges by explicit classification.\n\n### Constraints\nA declared limit never hides a defect inside the declared scope. The reviewer may dispute a limit; the dispute is a finding with its own classification. Declared limits are visible in the closeout and the attestation.\n\n### Non-goals\nLimiting the number of review rounds.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: A spec shall be able to declare `### Accepted limits` entries with stable ids (`L<N>`), each naming the assumption and why it is accepted.\n- R2: The review brief shall list the declared limits and instruct the reviewer to classify a finding that falls inside one as a follow-up naming the limit, or to dispute the limit explicitly.\n- R3: The conclusion and the attestation shall record which findings were classified under which limit.\n- R4: `lint-spec --ready-check` shall require accepted limits to be justified and the ADR, when present, to reference them.\n\n### Non-functional\nExisting behaviour outside the stated requirements is unchanged.\n\n### Security\nNo new input crosses a trust boundary without the existing confinement.\n\n### Compatibility\nRecords and indexes written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n`pose-mcp` (CLI and engine), the manual and its embedded copy.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_versions_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/governance_stats.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:review-declared-threat-model module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n\n\n### Accepted limits\n- L1: The limits are written by the implementer, so a limit can be broader than it should be. Accepted because the brief presents them as data to verify and a disputed limit is a blocking finding of its own.\n- L2: A spec edited after sealing changes its limits only through a new bundle. Accepted because the limits enter the plan digest, so an edit stales the review as any other change does.\n\n### API/contract changes\nA spec section `### Accepted limits` with `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` entries. The review plan and the bundle seal them as `accepted_limits` (omitted when none, so every other plan and bundle keeps its digest). A draft finding may carry `limit`; the attestation records it with disposition `within-limit`. Brief template `review-brief/11`. `lint-spec --ready-check` checks the section.\n\n\n### Data/storage changes\n`accepted_limits` in review plans and bundles and `limit` in draft and attestation findings, all omitted when empty.\n\n\n### Technical risks\nA limit could be used to wave a real defect through. It is the spec's own declaration, sealed into the plan digest and visible in the brief and the attestation; the reviewer may dispute it with a finding of its own, which then blocks as any finding does.\n\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Planning\n- [ ] Confirm the requirements against the current engine before starting.\n\n### Implementation\n- [ ] Implement each requirement with a test that fails before it.\n\n### Validation\n- [ ] `pose validate --carry` and `pose check --strict`.\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Basis: R1, R2, R3\nA finding inside a declared limit is neither an open finding nor an accepted risk: it gets its own disposition, `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity, requires a sealed limit and a reason, and records the limit's id. Accepted risks keep their severity bound; a limit is the spec's statement of scope, decided before the review.\n\n### Decision D2\n- Basis: R1, R4\nThe limits are sealed in the plan and the bundle, and in the plan digest, so the brief and the conclusion read them from what was reviewed, never from the current spec; both fields are omitted when empty so no earlier plan or bundle changes digest.\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n\n### Execution log\n- 2026-11-25: implemented R1–R4: `ParseAcceptedLimits`, the plan and bundle `accepted_limits`, the draft `limit` and the `within-limit` disposition, `review-brief/11` and the ready check. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out; the first digest test survived because the spec edit changed the scope digest too, and now compares the plan's digest with and without the limits.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 1 (rrn-d1680df442b23d12) requested changes. N1: in a scope with several specs (a milestone or a roadmap) each limit id is qualified by its spec as `<spec>#L<N>`, with the spec recorded, so a finding names exactly one sealed limit. N2: the reason must follow `Accepted because`, not only the marker. N3: an ADR names a limit only by the id as a word, so L10 does not name L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 2 (rrn-256db7e6eea5a6e2) found that punctuation after the marker passed as a reason and a one-letter first word failed. The reason must now hold at least two words with letters (`hasAcceptedLimitReason`), with a test that fails with the rule mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 3 (rrn-0af0d413761142d8) found that the marker matched inside a sentence, so 'Not accepted because ...' passed. The marker must now open a sentence (start of the text or after `.`, `;`, `:`, `!` or `?`), as the format `<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` states; the test fails with the boundary mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 4 (rrn-721e2a4c9bd37e2e) found that an entry with no assumption, or with an empty reason followed by other prose, passed. The entry is now read structurally: an assumption of at least two words, the marker opening a sentence, and a reason of at least two words within that same sentence. Each rule has a test case that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 5 (rrn-9f6867b3c3e42377): N1, a whitespace-only reason of a `within-limit` finding is refused; N2, the ready check fails closed on an ADR directory or file it cannot read, keeping the absent-directory case. Both have tests that fail with them mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 6 (rrn-a5b61489ac04873d) found the bundle's plan digest did not cover the sealed limits; it now does, omitted when empty so every other bundle keeps its digest. `TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits` checks both and fails with the field mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 7 (rrn-1236e104ff346a2d): N2, the ready check reads an ADR only as a regular file, without following a link and within 1 MiB, so a link to an endless source fails instead of hanging; the test fails with the bounded read mutated out. N1 (low) was classified by the reviewer within L1: the check is a format gate, and the substance of a limit is judged in review.\n- 2026-11-26: round 8 hit the Codex usage limit; by the maintainer's decision Claude Sonnet 5.5 reviews from here (a `claude-sonnet-5-5` profile in the delegation policy, the bundle resealed with the declared lead `anthropic/claude-opus-5-5` so the review is `different-model`; vendor independence is not achieved). Its round (rrn-b874a0e13bc62075) recorded N6 within L1, the first live use of `limit`, and requested changes: N1, a wrapped entry now keeps its continuation lines; N2, fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations; N3, `review-brief/11` (not yet published, so its digest was re-recorded) states the exception to rule (i) and lists `limit` among the finding fields; N4, an ADR cites the spec only by its slug as a whole name; N5, governance outcomes count `within-limit` findings in their own bucket. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out. `gofmt` over the package's tests also reformatted `delivery_surface_test.go` (formatting only), declared among the artifacts.\n- 2026-11-26: review round 9 (rrn-c3e24e7943c0693e, Claude Sonnet 5.5) requested changes. N1: the claim on `.pose/policy/delegation.json` is withdrawn: the reviewer profile is an operational change of this checkout, committed without the trailer, not a deliverable of the spec. N2: an entry is one Markdown list item: indented lines (also after a blank line) continue it, an unindented line or the next `-`/`*` item ends it, a wrapped `#` is no heading, and a nested list or a limit without its reason is reported and not sealed. N3: an ADR that links the spec's dated file cites it. N4 (fences close only with their own marker) and N5 (the governance text prints `within_limit`) are fixed in part; the rest are follow-ups. N6: the conclusion step of `review-brief/11` (re-recorded digest) says a follow-up with a sealed limit is recorded within it. N7 was recorded within L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n\n\n### Results summary\nThe full `pose-mcp` suite passes; the canonical result is refreshed with `pose validate --carry`.\n\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestAcceptedLimitsAreParsed, test:TestAcceptedLimitsReadWrappedEntriesAndSkipExamples, test:TestTheReviewPlanSealsTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestLimitsAcrossSpecsAreQualified, test:TestAnAcceptedLimitNeedsWordsAsItsReason\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestTheCurrentBriefListsTheAcceptedLimits\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAFindingWithinASealedLimitDoesNotBlockApproval, test:TestGovernanceOutcomesCountsJudgmentAndInvalidArtifacts\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestReadyCheckJudgesAcceptedLimits\n\n\n### Known gaps\nNone.\n\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\nSpecs declare accepted limits; the review plan and bundle seal them; `review-brief/11` lists them and tells the reviewer to classify a finding inside one with `limit`; the attestation records it `within-limit`, which does not block an approval; `lint-spec --ready-check` requires each limit to say why it is accepted and an ADR citing the spec to name it.\n\n\n### Files and modules changed\n`pose-mcp` (plan, bundle, conclusion, brief, lint), the manual in both locales and its embedded copies.\n\n\n### Validation executed\n`go test ./...` in `pose-mcp`; `pose check --strict`; `pose validate --carry --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json`.\n\n\n### Residual risks\nL1 and L2 above.\n\n\n### Follow-ups\n- [open] Make the accepted-limits reader refuse the Markdown it still misreads: an indented code example, a line opening with inline backticks, an unterminated `<!--` that hides later limits, and an HTML comment written inside backticks in a limit's text (review round 9, N4). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n- [open] Show within-limit findings in the text output of `pose review-check` and `pose closeout-check`, as the JSON already does (review round 9, N5). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"6bc0071e-c7c1-4cdb-8ea1-b0c44db5491c","timestamp":"2026-11-26T04:20:45.648Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\nslug: pose-review-declared-threat-model\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-23\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on: \nremediates:\npriority: 4\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: feature\nsurface: standard\ndelivers: capability:review-declared-threat-model\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nLet a spec declare the accepted residual limits of a guarantee it makes, and let the review brief treat findings inside those limits as follow-ups to classify, not blockers to chase.\n\n### Business value\nEach of the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks` found a more remote adversarial variant: concurrent HEAD movement, case folding, Unicode normalization, symlinks inside submodules. Every one was real, but no round could converge, because the spec had no place to say which environment the guarantee assumes. A cap on rounds would approve something unreviewed; a declared threat model converges by explicit classification.\n\n### Constraints\nA declared limit never hides a defect inside the declared scope. The reviewer may dispute a limit; the dispute is a finding with its own classification. Declared limits are visible in the closeout and the attestation.\n\n### Non-goals\nLimiting the number of review rounds.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: A spec shall be able to declare `### Accepted limits` entries with stable ids (`L<N>`), each naming the assumption and why it is accepted.\n- R2: The review brief shall list the declared limits and instruct the reviewer to classify a finding that falls inside one as a follow-up naming the limit, or to dispute the limit explicitly.\n- R3: The conclusion and the attestation shall record which findings were classified under which limit.\n- R4: `lint-spec --ready-check` shall require accepted limits to be justified and the ADR, when present, to reference them.\n\n### Non-functional\nExisting behaviour outside the stated requirements is unchanged.\n\n### Security\nNo new input crosses a trust boundary without the existing confinement.\n\n### Compatibility\nRecords and indexes written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n`pose-mcp` (CLI and engine), the manual and its embedded copy.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_versions_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/governance_stats.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:review-declared-threat-model module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n\n\n### Accepted limits\n- L1: The limits are written by the implementer, so a limit can be broader than it should be. Accepted because the brief presents them as data to verify and a disputed limit is a blocking finding of its own.\n- L2: A spec edited after sealing changes its limits only through a new bundle. Accepted because the limits enter the plan digest, so an edit stales the review as any other change does.\n\n### API/contract changes\nA spec section `### Accepted limits` with `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` entries. The review plan and the bundle seal them as `accepted_limits` (omitted when none, so every other plan and bundle keeps its digest). A draft finding may carry `limit`; the attestation records it with disposition `within-limit`. Brief template `review-brief/11`. `lint-spec --ready-check` checks the section.\n\n\n### Data/storage changes\n`accepted_limits` in review plans and bundles and `limit` in draft and attestation findings, all omitted when empty.\n\n\n### Technical risks\nA limit could be used to wave a real defect through. It is the spec's own declaration, sealed into the plan digest and visible in the brief and the attestation; the reviewer may dispute it with a finding of its own, which then blocks as any finding does.\n\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Planning\n- [ ] Confirm the requirements against the current engine before starting.\n\n### Implementation\n- [ ] Implement each requirement with a test that fails before it.\n\n### Validation\n- [ ] `pose validate --carry` and `pose check --strict`.\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Basis: R1, R2, R3\nA finding inside a declared limit is neither an open finding nor an accepted risk: it gets its own disposition, `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity, requires a sealed limit and a reason, and records the limit's id. Accepted risks keep their severity bound; a limit is the spec's statement of scope, decided before the review.\n\n### Decision D2\n- Basis: R1, R4\nThe limits are sealed in the plan and the bundle, and in the plan digest, so the brief and the conclusion read them from what was reviewed, never from the current spec; both fields are omitted when empty so no earlier plan or bundle changes digest.\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n\n### Execution log\n- 2026-11-25: implemented R1–R4: `ParseAcceptedLimits`, the plan and bundle `accepted_limits`, the draft `limit` and the `within-limit` disposition, `review-brief/11` and the ready check. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out; the first digest test survived because the spec edit changed the scope digest too, and now compares the plan's digest with and without the limits.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 1 (rrn-d1680df442b23d12) requested changes. N1: in a scope with several specs (a milestone or a roadmap) each limit id is qualified by its spec as `<spec>#L<N>`, with the spec recorded, so a finding names exactly one sealed limit. N2: the reason must follow `Accepted because`, not only the marker. N3: an ADR names a limit only by the id as a word, so L10 does not name L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 2 (rrn-256db7e6eea5a6e2) found that punctuation after the marker passed as a reason and a one-letter first word failed. The reason must now hold at least two words with letters (`hasAcceptedLimitReason`), with a test that fails with the rule mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 3 (rrn-0af0d413761142d8) found that the marker matched inside a sentence, so 'Not accepted because ...' passed. The marker must now open a sentence (start of the text or after `.`, `;`, `:`, `!` or `?`), as the format `<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` states; the test fails with the boundary mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 4 (rrn-721e2a4c9bd37e2e) found that an entry with no assumption, or with an empty reason followed by other prose, passed. The entry is now read structurally: an assumption of at least two words, the marker opening a sentence, and a reason of at least two words within that same sentence. Each rule has a test case that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 5 (rrn-9f6867b3c3e42377): N1, a whitespace-only reason of a `within-limit` finding is refused; N2, the ready check fails closed on an ADR directory or file it cannot read, keeping the absent-directory case. Both have tests that fail with them mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 6 (rrn-a5b61489ac04873d) found the bundle's plan digest did not cover the sealed limits; it now does, omitted when empty so every other bundle keeps its digest. `TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits` checks both and fails with the field mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 7 (rrn-1236e104ff346a2d): N2, the ready check reads an ADR only as a regular file, without following a link and within 1 MiB, so a link to an endless source fails instead of hanging; the test fails with the bounded read mutated out. N1 (low) was classified by the reviewer within L1: the check is a format gate, and the substance of a limit is judged in review.\n- 2026-11-26: round 8 hit the Codex usage limit; by the maintainer's decision Claude Sonnet 5.5 reviews from here (a `claude-sonnet-5-5` profile in the delegation policy, the bundle resealed with the declared lead `anthropic/claude-opus-5-5` so the review is `different-model`; vendor independence is not achieved). Its round (rrn-b874a0e13bc62075) recorded N6 within L1, the first live use of `limit`, and requested changes: N1, a wrapped entry now keeps its continuation lines; N2, fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations; N3, `review-brief/11` (not yet published, so its digest was re-recorded) states the exception to rule (i) and lists `limit` among the finding fields; N4, an ADR cites the spec only by its slug as a whole name; N5, governance outcomes count `within-limit` findings in their own bucket. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out. `gofmt` over the package's tests also reformatted `delivery_surface_test.go` (formatting only), declared among the artifacts.\n- 2026-11-26: review round 9 (rrn-c3e24e7943c0693e, Claude Sonnet 5.5) requested changes. N1: the claim on `.pose/policy/delegation.json` is withdrawn: the reviewer profile is an operational change of this checkout, committed without the trailer, not a deliverable of the spec. N2: an entry is one Markdown list item: indented lines (also after a blank line) continue it, an unindented line or the next `-`/`*` item ends it, a wrapped `#` is no heading, and a nested list or a limit without its reason is reported and not sealed. N3: an ADR that links the spec's dated file cites it. N4 (fences close only with their own marker) and N5 (the governance text prints `within_limit`) are fixed in part; the rest are follow-ups. N6: the conclusion step of `review-brief/11` (re-recorded digest) says a follow-up with a sealed limit is recorded within it. N7 was recorded within L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n\n\n### Results summary\nThe full `pose-mcp` suite passes; the canonical result is refreshed with `pose validate --carry`.\n\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestAcceptedLimitsAreParsed, test:TestAcceptedLimitsReadWrappedEntriesAndSkipExamples, test:TestTheReviewPlanSealsTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestLimitsAcrossSpecsAreQualified, test:TestAnAcceptedLimitNeedsWordsAsItsReason\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestTheCurrentBriefListsTheAcceptedLimits\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAFindingWithinASealedLimitDoesNotBlockApproval, test:TestGovernanceOutcomesCountsJudgmentAndInvalidArtifacts\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestReadyCheckJudgesAcceptedLimits\n\n\n### Known gaps\nNone.\n\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\nSpecs declare accepted limits; the review plan and bundle seal them; `review-brief/11` lists them and tells the reviewer to classify a finding inside one with `limit`; the attestation records it `within-limit`, which does not block an approval; `lint-spec --ready-check` requires each limit to say why it is accepted and an ADR citing the spec to name it.\n\n\n### Files and modules changed\n`pose-mcp` (plan, bundle, conclusion, brief, lint), the manual in both locales and its embedded copies.\n\n\n### Validation executed\n`go test ./...` in `pose-mcp`; `pose check --strict`; `pose validate --carry --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json`.\n\n\n### Residual risks\nL1 and L2 above.\n\n\n### Follow-ups\n- [open] Make the accepted-limits reader refuse the Markdown it still misreads: an indented code example, a line opening with inline backticks, an unterminated `<!--` that hides later limits, and an HTML comment written inside backticks in a limit's text (review round 9, N4). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n- [open] Show within-limit findings in the text output of `pose review-check` and `pose closeout-check`, as the JSON already does (review round 9, N5). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Ep4BBCfoQ9X5A4aqq5RewY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQu4j3sdCH7QzYPcS4BZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LAe3PnAg4mLkNpWi9oc5tv","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git diff 3f63a1bb432801957ddb35354961f1825658531a 6e79f8af -- pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go POSE.md locales"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15487,"cache_read_input_tokens":43786,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15487},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"dd086a87-acb9-4b6b-829f-1bd1fc184b28","timestamp":"2026-11-26T04:20:47.474Z","request_id":"req_011ChQu4isx3b4nL9szv4mj9","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LAe3PnAg4mLkNpWi9oc5tv":{"command":"git diff 3f63a1bb432801957ddb35354961f1825658531a 6e79f8af -- pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go POSE.md locales"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LAe3PnAg4mLkNpWi9oc5tv","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (62.7KB). Full output saved to: /home/go/.claude/projects/-var-tmp-pose-work-go-pose-review-run-1702975921-worktree/[private-session]/tool-results/b07wcos16.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\ndiff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 3f2055de..57f95eef 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -420,7 +420,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `usage` — aggregates automatic, project-scoped CLI and MCP usage events: calls, execution/semantic outcomes, latency, findings observed and stable finding lifecycle (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agents never maintain counters. Events are best-effort, local-only and stored outside the tracked worktree; raw arguments, output, paths, project/user identity and finding IDs are never persisted. `--since-days 0` includes all history; filter with `--tool` or `--surface`; MCP equivalent: `pose_usage`. This is product-usage evidence, not a DORA delivery outcome or an individual productivity score.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingest identity-free schema-v2 delivery events and calculate metrics for explicit production environments (`--environment`, default `production`). Deployments require `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidents require an environment; recovery includes only resolved `--caused-by-deployment` incidents. `adoption-metrics` aggregates specifications, roadmaps, and validation lifecycle rates.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"f960f18b-0d53-4f64-b3f2-83f1473b89bf","timestamp":"2026-11-26T04:20:47.536Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 3f2055de..57f95eef 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -420,7 +420,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `usage` — aggregates automatic, project-scoped CLI and MCP usage events: calls, execution/semantic outcomes, latency, findings observed and stable finding lifecycle (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agents never maintain counters. Events are best-effort, local-only and stored outside the tracked worktree; raw arguments, output, paths, project/user identity and finding IDs are never persisted. `--since-days 0` includes all history; filter with `--tool` or `--surface`; MCP equivalent: `pose_usage`. This is product-usage evidence, not a DORA delivery outcome or an individual productivity score.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingest identity-free schema-v2 delivery events and calculate metrics for explicit production environments (`--environment`, default `production`). Deployments require `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidents require an environment; recovery includes only resolved `--caused-by-deployment` incidents. `adoption-metrics` aggregates specifications, roadmaps, and validation lifecycle rates.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — read-only, offline catalog inspection (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Matches directory entries against the maintained profile catalog (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — and **.NET**), reporting per profile: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (multiple managers present resolve by declared priority), `confidence` (`medium` under conflict), and whether the prerequisite native tool is on `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, never by executing a project file. Detected markers feed `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` the same way Node/Go/Rust/Java already do; the actual checks run through [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json) `python`/`dotnet` stacks. Python manager priority is expressed with `when.fileNotExistsAny` (skip when any higher-priority lockfile/marker exists) alongside the existing `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` predicates.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle. `discover`, `integrate` and `tech-debt` take `--no-persist` to print their result without writing `.pose/assessments/` or `.pose/state/`; in the sealed checkout of a delegated review (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) they never persist, `init`, `snapshot` and `request` refuse, and the review plan runs them with `--no-persist`, so a reviewer running a planned assessment does not fail its own run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — environment, dependency, native runtime, and POSE instance health diagnostics, plus governance that would otherwise fail only downstream: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (a selected profile demands a class no check may emit), `validate.class-producers` (a criterion accepts only classes no registered check produces), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks with no `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set`, and `<policy>.policy-keys` for nine policies (keys the engine does not read, i.e. a misspelled setting that silently does nothing).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — idempotent maintenance (list/archive/purge). Mutations require `--apply`. Reports housekeeping **never touches `history/`**: the JSONL is the source of truth for `recurrence-check` and `report`'s temporal comparisons. Defaults: stale = 120d, archive purge = 365d.\n - `amend` — append-only spec amendment history (`amendments.jsonl` beside a folder spec's `spec.md`, or `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` beside a flat dated spec; the spec is resolved through the store, so both layouts amend alike). `--baseline` snapshots every R-ID hash; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` acknowledges a material change; `--list` renders history and pending acknowledgments. On `done` specs with a history, `lint-spec` rejects any requirement whose current text is not acknowledged by an event — specs cannot be silently rewritten after evidence. `--nodes [--json]` prints the versioned contract-node projection: requirements, assumptions and decisions with namespace, content hash, state and relations. With `contract_nodes_version: 1` adopted in the review policy, `--baseline` writes a schema-2 R/A/D snapshot, `--ids` accepts A/D, `--change transition` acknowledges an allowed state change, `editorial` is refused when a node changed state or relations, and the gate also rejects an active decision resting on an invalidated or withdrawn assumption. Without the capability the gate stays requirement-only and a schema-2 event is refused; schema-1 logs keep their meaning.\n@@ -1071,7 +1071,13 @@ Preview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then\n \n Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n \n-**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/7` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/7` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n+**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/11` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A conclusion that requests changes or rejects is recorded even while the working tree already holds edits of the sealed subject, since it is a fact about the bundle it reviewed; an approval refused only because the working tree no longer holds its bundle's subject is reported without a record, and the same run concludes once the tree matches. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/11` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n+\n+**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n+\n+**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, which may wrap over several lines (fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n+\n+**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object that the dispatch terminates after the run, on a timeout and, through the job's limit, when the dispatcher ends. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n \n **Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\n \ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 3c20d024..f7b6d52d 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -424,7 +424,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `usage` — agrega automaticamente eventos locais e por projeto de uso da CLI e do MCP: chamadas, outcomes de execução/semântica, latência, findings observados e ciclo de vida estável (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agentes nunca mantêm contadores. Os eventos são best-effort, somente locais e ficam fora da árvore versionada; argumentos, saída, paths, identidade de projeto/usuário e IDs crus de findings nunca são persistidos. `--since-days 0` inclui todo o histórico; filtre com `--tool` ou `--surface`; equivalente MCP: `pose_usage`. É evidência de uso do produto, não outcome DORA nem score individual de produtividade.\n - `record-deployment` / `record-incident` / `dora-metrics` / `adoption-metrics` — ingerem eventos de entrega schema v2 sem identidade individual e calculam métricas para um ambiente de produção explícito (`--environment`, padrão `production`). Deployments exigem `--deployment-kind planned|rework`; incidentes exigem ambiente; recovery inclui apenas incidentes resolvidos com `--caused-by-deployment`. `adoption-metrics` agrega taxas de especificações, roadmaps e validação de ciclo de vida.\n - `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — inspeção de catálogo read-only e offline (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Casa entradas de diretório contra o catálogo mantido de perfis (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — e **.NET**), reportando por perfil: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (múltiplos managers presentes resolvem por prioridade declarada), `confidence` (`medium` sob conflito) e se a ferramenta nativa pré-requisito está no `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, nunca executando um arquivo do projeto. Markers detectados alimentam `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` do mesmo jeito que Node/Go/Rust/Java já fazem; os checks propriamente ditos rodam pelas stacks `python`/`dotnet` da [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json). A prioridade de manager Python é expressa com `when.fileNotExistsAny` (pular quando existir qualquer lockfile/marker de prioridade maior) ao lado dos predicados `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` já existentes.\n-- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities.\n+- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities. `discover`, `integrate` e `tech-debt` aceitam `--no-persist` para imprimir o resultado sem gravar `.pose/assessments/` nem `.pose/state/`; no checkout selado de uma review delegada (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) eles nunca gravam, `init`, `snapshot` e `request` recusam, e o plano de review os roda com `--no-persist`, para que um revisor que roda um assessment planejado não derrube o próprio run.\n - `doctor [--fix]` — diagnósticos de ambiente, dependências, runtime nativo e saúde da instância POSE, além de governança que só falharia mais adiante: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (um profile selecionado exige uma classe que nenhum check pode emitir), `validate.class-producers` (um critério aceita apenas classes que nenhum check registrado produz), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks sem `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set` e `<policy>.policy-keys` para nove políticas (chaves que o motor não lê, ou seja, uma configuração com erro de digitação que silenciosamente não faz nada).\n - `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — manutenção idempotente (listar/arquivar/expurgar). Mutações exigem `--apply`. O housekeeping de reports **nunca toca em `history/`**: o JSONL é a fonte de verdade para `recurrence-check` e comparações temporais de `report`. Defaults: stale = 120d, purga de arquivo = 365d.\n - `amend` — histórico append-only de emendas da spec (`amendments.jsonl` ao lado do `spec.md` de uma spec em pasta, ou `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` ao lado de uma spec flat datada; a spec é resolvida pelo store, então os dois layouts recebem emendas do mesmo modo). `--baseline` fotografa o hash de cada R-ID; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` reconhece uma mudança material; `--list` renderiza o histórico e os reconhecimentos pendentes. Em specs `done` com histórico, o `lint-spec` rejeita todo requisito cujo texto atual não esteja reconhecido por um evento — specs não podem ser reescritas em silêncio depois da evidência. `--nodes [--json]` mostra a projeção versionada de contract nodes: requisitos, premissas e decisões com namespace, hash de conteúdo, estado e relações. Com `contract_nodes_version: 1` adotado na policy de review, `--baseline` grava um snapshot R/A/D schema 2, `--ids` aceita A/D, `--change transition` reconhece uma mudança de estado permitida, `editorial` é recusado quando o nó mudou estado ou relações, e o gate também rejeita decisão ativa apoiada em premissa invalidada ou retirada. Sem a capability o gate segue só com requisitos e um evento schema 2 é recusado; logs schema 1 mantêm o significado.\n@@ -1068,7 +1068,13 @@ Visualize `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <nome> [--ttl <duração>]` e\n \n Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicitação completa de `pose review attest`. O issuer deriva do run os vínculos de principal/execução e assina a referência exata. Critérios, tools, evidências e preflight continuam obrigatórios. Draft negativo ou ambíguo não apoia aprovação; a atestação não pode anteceder o término do run ou sua prova assinada. Provas de adjudication/smoke vinculam seus kinds e não servem como atestações de review. Validar/aplicar automaticamente conclusões completas e tipadas pertence à capability, não à assinatura do run.\n \n-**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/7` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da prime","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/go/.claude/projects/-var-tmp-pose-work-go-pose-review-run-1702975921-worktree/[private-session]/tool-results/b07wcos16.txt","persistedOutputSize":64213},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LAe3PnAg4mLkNpWi9oc5tv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
